 the  numbers were dropping this past  weekend's ratings were the lowest yet  they had done well from an attendance  standpoint in st louis and seattle i  think they need to move the la team to  san diego and the new york team to  oakland frankly because that concept of  being a  a scorned nfl city worked in st louis  they really need though to think about  the places they can put these teams  where it will resonate with the fan base  and i think it makes a ton of sense to  be in cities that don't have nfl teams  one of the eight teams is in a non-nfl  city and that's st louis and you know i  i think you try to find a city that is  big enough  that you believe will support you san  antonio why they got teams in houston  and dallas so they aren't going to put  one in san antonio but why not put one  of those two teams in san antonio  instead if you find a city that can  rally around a team that doesn't have  anything to otherwise rally around  that's how you get people to come out  it's that simple if they're going to cap  attendance around 20 25 000 because if  they play in these big arenas they're  only going to play in the lower bowl or  only seat people in the lower bowl then  then look for places like that where you  can get 25 to 30 000 on a regular basis  especially since it looks a lot better  on tv and i enjoyed the game and there  were things about it that i liked i mean  people think oh you hate the nf the xfl  so just because i'm pointing out the  things that aren't necessarily positive  doesn't mean i hate the xfl i watched it  every weekend it's football and it's on  tv i love the kickoff i like the  creativity of the extra point i have  ideas for making that better we never  got to see an overtime game  unfortunately because they took our idea  for the overtime two-point conversion  shootout but uh yeah i i i hope it does  well  i've been skeptical though i don't want  to fall in love with it and have it go  away and i'm still skeptical to be back  next year but i hope it will be  and uh you know we'll see  we'll see if they can attract some star  players we'll see if they have a plan  for keeping star players you know pj  walker one of the guys that's likely to  be gone for the nfl will he be back in  the xfl next year we'll see  but but i agree with you it'll be back  and i think next year  the pressure will be on to really  establish a foothold and maintain it and  next year we'll find out whether or not  the xfl can hold its own against uh  the the college basketball tournaments  that were going to suck away a lot of  their audience  yeah and you know the one other thing i  would like to see the xfl do is  something they they talked about doing  and then didn't really do  um which is  sign college freshmen and sophomores who  aren't eligible for the nfl draft and  i don't think they'll ever  be able to offer the kind of money that  a trevor lawrence would take because he  knows hey i'm a year away from getting  tens of millions in the nfl but you know  go go to a wide receiver who made some  big plays in a bowl game who's a  freshman or sophomore and say hey we'll  sign you to a hundred thousand dollars  right now  and make it crystal clear in your  contract that as soon as your nfl draft  eligible you owe us nothing you can  still go to the nfl but we'll pay you a  lot more money than you're making in  college  and i think that would be an interesting  experiment  for  whether they could sign some of the the  second tier not not the guys who are  sure they're going to be first-round  picks in a year but some of the guys who  look like maybe mid-round picks who are  freshmen and sophomores and approach  them about playing in the xfl for a year  or two i think that would be a really  interesting way  for the xfl to position itself as the  developmental league and as a league  that got some guys who we've seen before  oh yeah i remember watching the orange  bowl and score seeing that guy score two  touchdowns t